 ],
 "literal": "Milk having taken when fed, he would not drink, and so / without being angry, a raised small stick fearing / with distress he would suffer indeed. Now, / spotted-colored male elephants having slain without stopping, / the son of the brave warrior who fell on a previous day, / 'I will not think of retreating' saying, with an arrow in his wound, / like a horse's mane, with a tuft of hair, / upon his shield lies he of the sparse beard.",
 "translation": "In the past, when milk was offered to him, he would refuse to drink, and he would shrink in distress, fearing even a small stick raised without anger. But now, this son of the brave warrior who fell in battle the other day has slain spotted elephants without stopping. Saying 'I will not think of retreating,' with an arrow piercing his wound, he lies dead upon his shield, his sparse beard matching his tuft of hair that looks like a horse's mane.",
 "modern": "முன்பு, பால் கொடுத்து ஊட்டினாலும் அவன் குடிக்க மாட்டான். கோபமின்றி ஓங்கிய சிறு கோலைக் கண்டு அஞ்சி வருந்துவான். ஆனால் இப்போதோ, முந்தைய நாளில் போரில் வீழ்ந்த வீரனின் மகனாகிய இவன், புள்ளிகளையுடைய யானைகளைக் கொன்று குவித்தான். 'பின்வாங்க நினைக்க மாட்டேன்' என்று கூறி, உடலில் அம்பு தைத்த புண்ணோடு, குதிரையின் பிடரி மயிர் போன்ற குடுமியையும், சிறிய தாடியையும் உடையவனாய், தன் கேடயத்தின் மேல் இறந்து கிடக்கிறான்.",
 "explanation": "This poem belongs to the Tumpai tiṇai (battle) and the theme is Nūḻilāṭṭu (the heroic stand of a single warrior). The poet Ponmuṭiyār describes a young warrior who has fallen in battle. In his childhood, he was so tender and timid that he would refuse milk and cry in fear at the sight of a small stick raised without any real anger. Now, as the son of a brave warrior who died in battle the previous day, he has slain many war elephants. Refusing to retreat, he lies dead upon his shield with an arrow in his wound, his youthful sparse beard and horse-mane-like tuft of hair marking his transition from a delicate boy to a heroic martyr.",
 "model": "gemini-3.5-flash",
